index 0000000..f21abcb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+# Deploy Fizzy to one server with Kamal
+
+You need a Linux server with Docker, SSH access (Kamal defaults to `root`), and
+ports 80 and 443 open. Point your hostname's DNS at the server before deploying
+so Kamal Proxy can obtain a Let's Encrypt certificate. On your workstation, you
+need Docker with Buildx and the repo's Ruby and gems (`bundle install`).
+
+1. In `config/deploy.yml`, replace `fizzy.example.com` with your hostname.
+   Replace `your-docker-hub-user` in both `image` and `registry.username`.
+   Change `builder.arch` to `arm64` if that's what your server runs. If you
+   connect as a different SSH user, set `ssh.user` to a user with Docker access.
+2. Set `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`
+   for your mail provider. The template expects authenticated SMTP with STARTTLS
+   (typically port 587); use a sender address your provider authorizes.
+3. Export `KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD` (a Docker Hub token with permission to push
+   the image), `SMTP_PASSWORD`, and `SECRET_KEY_BASE` in your deploy shell.
+   Generate the Rails secret once with `openssl rand -hex 64`, keep it in your
+   secret store, and reuse it on each deploy. Never commit the values.
+4. Commit your config changes, then run `bin/kamal setup` from the checkout.
+   Once it finishes, visit `https://your-host/signup` to create your account.
+   For subsequent releases, run `bin/kamal deploy`.
+
+Kamal Proxy terminates HTTPS and forwards traffic to the app container on port
+80. The container prepares the SQLite databases at startup and runs Solid Queue
+inside Puma, so no separate job container or database server is needed.
+
+The Docker volume `fizzy_storage` holds all SQLite databases and uploaded files.
+Kamal reuses it across deploys on the same server. Back up the volume (using a
+consistent SQLite backup or stopping the app for the backup) before moving hosts
+or making destructive changes. Deleting the volume deletes your data.
